==English==

===Etymology===
From the days of [[flintlock]] firearms, where the main charge was intended to be fired by a small charge of gunpowder in the [[priming]] pan. If the resultant fire did not pass through the [[touch hole]] and ignite the main charge, it produced noise and smoke, but no substantial effect, and was termed a ''flash in the pan''. Sometimes called ''fluff in the pan'', the term refers to any ineffectual, short, spasmodic effort that dies in the attempt, such as an explosion of priming in the [[lockpan]] of a gun while the gun itself does not go off.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-au-flash in the pan.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|flashes in the pan|flash in the pans}}

# A [[transient]] [[occurrence]] with no [[long-term]] effect.
#: {{ux|en|The concept turned out to be merely a '''flash in the pan''' and is no longer used.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=http://www.theguardian.com/education/2015/apr/04/school-dinners-revolution-10-years-on|title=The new dinner lady: 10 years on, can an Ottolenghi chef prove Jamie Oliver's revolution wasn't a flash in the pan?|author=Sally Weale|date=2015-04-04|work=The Guardian|passage=The new dinner lady: 10 years on, can an Ottolenghi chef prove Jamie Oliver's revolution wasn't a '''flash in the pan'''? [title]}}
#* {{seeCites|en}}
# A [[career]] notable for [[early]] [[success]] not followed by significant accomplishment.
#: {{ux|en|He was named best new director of 1940, but his career was a '''flash in the pan'''.}}

